{
  "assessment": "The change modifies `sla.py` to use `st.is_terminal(job.status)` instead of an explicit check for `(DONE, FAILED)`. This relies on `status.py` correctly identifying `CANCELLED` as a terminal status (where the clock stops). If the status module was updated with the new vocabulary but the logic in `is_terminal` was not correspondingly updated to include it, cancelled jobs will still be treated as active. The comment asserts cancellation is included, but without seeing `status.py`, this remains an unverified dependency that could perpetuate the SLA reporting error.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "If st.is_terminal excludes CANCELLED, finished_at returns now (current time), causing cancelled jobs to accrue infinite SLA time.",
      "at": "/jobs/sla.py",
      "call": "python3 run.py data/jobs.json",
      "defect": "Reliance on `st.is_terminal` for determining if the clock stops on a job. If the status module does not include CANCELLED in its definition of terminal statuses, this function will return `now` instead of `ended_at`, causing cancelled jobs to continue accruing SLA time indefinitely.",
      "expected": "Cancelled jobs should stop accruing SLA time at their `ended_at` timestamp."
    }
  ]
}
